    {
      "id": "industry.agent",
      "slug": "agent",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Agent",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/agent/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.agent.md",
      "summaryHtml": "An agent is what you deal with when work gets done: it reads, acts and reports in the world where a model alone cannot.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>An agent is what you deal with when work gets done: it reads, acts and reports in the world where a model alone cannot.</p>\n<p>An agent is a <a href=\"/glossary/model/\">model</a> working through an <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a>. The model decides each step and the harness carries it out. Vendors name an agent after its harness, so a product name can mean the agent or its harness, by context.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>The definitions under the KI backpack's <code>subagents/</code> folder describe roles an agent takes when it is run as a subagent, not agents of their own; the <a href=\"/glossary/subagent/\">subagent</a> card places them.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>An <a href=\"/glossary/agentic-system/\">agentic system</a> is any system in which models direct some of their own steps: the umbrella over every term here.</p>",
      "instances": [
        {
          "slug": "github-copilot-cloud-agent",
          "name": "GitHub Copilot cloud agent",
          "vendor": "GitHub"
        },
        {
          "slug": "jules",
          "name": "Jules",
          "vendor": "Google"
        },
        {
          "slug": "microsoft-365-copilot",
          "name": "Microsoft 365 Copilot",
          "vendor": "Microsoft"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.agentic-system",
      "slug": "agentic-system",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Agentic system",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/agentic-system/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.agentic-system.md",
      "summaryHtml": "An agentic system lets work get done by models that decide some of their own steps, rather than by code that fixes every step in advance.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>An agentic system lets work get done by models that decide some of their own steps, rather than by code that fixes every step in advance.</p>\n<p>An agentic system is any system in which <a href=\"/glossary/model/\">models</a> direct some of their own steps and tool use. It is built in two ways, often together: <strong>workflows</strong>, where code sets the path and orchestrates models and tools along it, and <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agents</a>, where the model directs its own process. It is the umbrella over every term in this graph, and the cards that follow build it up from one agent's run to agents working together, in one system or across several.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>Knowledge Islands' own agents, with the skills and tools they work through, make up one agentic system in this sense.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">session</a> is one run of an agent.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.session",
      "slug": "session",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Session",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/session/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.session.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A session keeps one run of an agent together, its context, its record and its place, so the work can be followed and resumed.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A session keeps one run of an agent together, its context, its record and its place, so the work can be followed and resumed.</p>\n<p>A session is one run of an <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agent</a>: a conversation with its own context, its own id and the folder it works in. It is made of <strong>turns</strong>, each a request and the agent's work on it. The <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a> runs the session and keeps its <strong>transcript</strong>, the record of every turn, so the session can be resumed later with its context intact.</p>\n<p>A session is also made of <strong>threads</strong>. The agent's own line of work is its <strong>primary thread</strong>, and each <a href=\"/glossary/subagent/\">subagent</a> it starts works in a thread of its own within the session. A thread is a part of a session, never another word for one.</p>\n<h3>Specifications</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Agent Client Protocol (ACP):</strong> its <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp-session\">session</a>, a close match.</li><li><strong>Agent Host Protocol (AHP):</strong> its <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-session\">session and the chats inside it</a>. Only a chat spawned by a tool call is a thread in this card's sense, a subagent's; a chat the user makes, a fork or a side chat is a conversation of its own, nearer an ACP session.</li></ul>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>The Knowledge Islands system already says session in this sense, as in the <code>ki-acquire</code> skill's agent-session acquisition and the housekeeping skills' session inventories, and has no convention of its own to map onto it.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"/glossary/subagent/\">subagent</a> is an agent started by another agent to do part of its work, in a thread of its own.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.subagent",
      "slug": "subagent",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Subagent",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/subagent/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.subagent.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A subagent lets an agent hand part of its work to another agent under its control and take back the result.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A subagent lets an agent hand part of its work to another agent under its control and take back the result.</p>\n<p>A subagent is an <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agent</a> subordinate to another agent, started by it to do part of its work. It acts wholly within the controlling agent's boundaries and under its control, and returns its result to it. It does not interact with other agents: the controlling agent is the point of interaction. Each subagent works in a thread of its own within the <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">session</a>, beside the controlling agent's primary thread.</p>\n<p>Harnesses vary in how they implement subagents, such as whether a subagent may start subagents of its own, and that is not part of the definition. Agent teams and remote agents reached over the Agent2Agent protocol (A2A) are agents in their own right, not subagents.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>The definitions in the KI backpack's <code>subagents/</code> folder describe roles a subagent takes, such as a reviewer or an explorer; each is run as a subagent of the controlling agent. The <code>ki-subagents</code> skill and its bindings say how to run them in a given agent host.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"/glossary/skill/\">skill</a> extends an agent harness with knowledge and procedures.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.skill",
      "slug": "skill",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Skill",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/skill/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.skill.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A skill is a common way to extend any <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a> with knowledge and procedures, so the same capability works across harnesses and costs the agent little until it is needed.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A skill is a common way to extend any <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a> with knowledge and procedures, so the same capability works across harnesses and costs the agent little until it is needed.</p>\n<p>It keeps that cost low through <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-agent-skills-progressive-disclosure\">progressive disclosure</a>: the agent holds only the skill's name and description until a task calls for it, then reads its instructions, then the files it bundles. A skill is built as a folder holding a <code>SKILL.md</code>, under the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-agent-skills\">Agent Skills specification</a>.</p>\n<p>Tools, instruction files and plugins are not skills: a tool is an action the harness carries out, an instruction file is always loaded, and a plugin is a bundle that can carry skills.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>Every skill in the KI backpack's <code>skills/</code> folder is a skill in this sense. The <code>ki-skills</code> skill governs how they are written, and adds the system's own front-matter metadata, such as <code>ki-kind</code>, within what the specification allows.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>An <a href=\"/glossary/environment/\">execution environment</a> is the bounded place where a session's tool calls run.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.environment",
      "slug": "environment",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Execution environment",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/environment/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.environment.md",
      "summaryHtml": "An execution environment, or environment for short, sets the bounds of an <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agent's</a> work: where its actions run, and what they can reach.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>An execution environment, or environment for short, sets the bounds of an <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agent's</a> work: where its actions run, and what they can reach.</p>\n<p>An execution environment is the bounded place where a <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">session's</a> tool calls actually run: the files, processes, network and credentials they can reach. It has a location and an isolation. It may be provided by the agent host, the <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a> or the vendor, but it is none of them. A worktree is a working folder, not an environment.</p>\n<p>Its location is where it runs: a <a href=\"/glossary/machine/\">machine</a>, physical or virtual, or a vendor's cloud, on machines you cannot identify, as Claude Code on the web and Codex Cloud run. Its isolation is how far it is fenced off there: from none, a session running directly on a machine within what its configuration allows, to a harness's sandbox or a container. Location and isolation are two properties, never one value; &quot;host environment&quot; is not used (HOST-SENSES).</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>The machine, the execution environment and the agent host are recorded apart, each a field of its own that can be filtered on by itself, never folded into one compound value (EXEC-ENV-VALUES): the Cairn store's roster records an agent <a href=\"/glossary/actor/\">actor's</a> machine as <code>ki-rig</code>, its execution environment as <code>ki-environment</code> and its agent host as <code>ki-host</code>. A compact display may show them together, while the stored values stay separate.</p>\n<p><code>ki-environment</code> records isolation only, one of four values (EXEC-ENV-NAMES):</p>\n<ul><li><code>none</code>: checked, and running directly on the machine within what its configuration allows;</li><li><code>harness-sandbox</code>: the agent harness's own sandbox;</li><li><code>container</code>: a container;</li><li><code>vendor-sandbox</code>: a sandbox the vendor provides, as for a cloud session.</li></ul>\n<p><code>null</code> means unknown, never <code>none</code>. Location stays in <code>ki-rig</code>: the machine's name, a virtual machine counting as a machine, or <code>cloud</code> for a vendor's machines that cannot be identified. &quot;Remote&quot; is not stored, since it depends on where one looks from. The compact display shows the machine, then the environment unless it is <code>none</code>, then the agent host, as <code>sol · orca</code> or <code>sol · container · orca</code>.</p>\n<p>Orca's own &quot;environment&quot;, a paired remote Orca runtime, is not an execution environment; <a href=\"https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/vendors/orca/orca.environment.md\">its card</a> relates it to this one.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"/glossary/machine/\">machine</a> is the computer an execution environment runs on.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.machine",
      "slug": "machine",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Machine",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/machine/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.machine.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A machine is the computer, physical or virtual, that <a href=\"/glossary/environment/\">execution environments</a> run on.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A machine is the computer, physical or virtual, that <a href=\"/glossary/environment/\">execution environments</a> run on.</p>\n<p>Networking calls a machine a host, and OpenAI and Google write &quot;host&quot; or &quot;host machine&quot; for one; here it is always machine, so host never stands alone (HOST-SENSES). A virtual machine is a machine; a container or sandbox inside one is an execution environment, not a machine.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"/glossary/rig/\">rig</a>, Knowledge Islands' word for a machine it manages through Rig, such as terra or sol, maps onto a machine.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>An <a href=\"/glossary/agent-host/\">agent host</a> runs on a machine and owns the sessions.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.agent-host",
      "slug": "agent-host",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Agent host",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/agent-host/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.agent-host.md",
      "summaryHtml": "An agent host lets <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">sessions</a> outlive, and be shared across, the windows used to see them.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>An agent host lets <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">sessions</a> outlive, and be shared across, the windows used to see them.</p>\n<p>An agent host is the application or process, running on a <a href=\"/glossary/machine/\">machine</a>, that runs <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harnesses</a> and owns their sessions, independently of the clients that display them. It may provide its sessions' <a href=\"/glossary/environment/\">execution environment</a>, but it is not one. The definition is the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-agent-host\">Agent Host Protocol's agent host</a> (AGENT-HOST-CITE): AHP calls it &quot;a portable, standalone sessions server&quot; (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/what-is-ahp.html\">what is AHP</a>), and the harness is what &quot;the host wraps&quot; (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/mcp.html\">MCP servers</a>).</p>\n<p>Examples: Orca, Claude Desktop, the ChatGPT desktop app, the Antigravity app and VS Code's Agent Host (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/implementations.html\">M12</a>). A harness run by itself, such as Claude Code or Codex in a terminal, is its own agent host and client in one process.</p>\n<p>The leaders have no formal word: Anthropic's &quot;surface&quot; covers agent host and client together, and OpenAI's &quot;host&quot; is the machine.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>On terra, Orca's runtime lives inside the Orca app; <code>orca serve</code> runs the same runtime headless, as on sol.</p>\n<p>Knowledge Islands names an agent host by one of six values: <code>orca</code>; <code>claude-desktop</code>; <code>chatgpt-desktop</code>; <code>chatgpt-voice</code>, the ChatGPT app in voice mode; <code>terminal</code>, a harness run by itself; and <code>ide</code>, an editor extension. The Cairn store's roster records each agent <a href=\"/glossary/actor/\">actor</a>'s agent host as <code>ki-host</code>, beside its agent harness as <code>ki-harness</code> (PRESENCE-FIELDS a), so the two are never folded into one: Claude Code inside Orca is <code>orca</code> with <code>claude-code</code>, and Claude Code alone in a terminal is <code>terminal</code> with <code>claude-code</code>.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"/glossary/client/\">client</a> is a window onto a session an agent host owns.</p>",
      "instances": [
        {
          "slug": "antigravity",
          "name": "Antigravity",
          "vendor": "Google"
        },
        {
          "slug": "chatgpt",
