    {
      "id": "industry.cross-session-messaging",
      "slug": "cross-session-messaging",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Cross-session messaging",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/cross-session-messaging/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.cross-session-messaging.md",
      "summaryHtml": "Cross-session messaging lets one session tell another what it needs to know while both are running, such as a finding, a status or a decision, without anyone copying it across.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>Cross-session messaging lets one session tell another what it needs to know while both are running, such as a finding, a status or a decision, without anyone copying it across.</p>\n<p>Cross-session messaging is one <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">session</a> sending a plain-text message to another, on the same <a href=\"/glossary/machine/\">machine</a> or beyond it. A message is text the sending agent writes, never its history or files. The receiving agent reads it between tool calls during a turn, or, when its session is idle, a new turn starts with it. A <strong>peer</strong> is the session at the other end; there is no separate peer agent. Each session keeps its own permissions, and a message from a peer is never the user's consent.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>Sessions on Knowledge Islands' rigs that message one another, such as a session handing the housekeeper on its rig a piece of work, are peers in this sense.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>An <a href=\"/glossary/agent-team/\">agent team</a> is several sessions working on one piece of work together, messaging each other and a team lead.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.agent-team",
      "slug": "agent-team",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Agent team",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/agent-team/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.agent-team.md",
      "summaryHtml": "An agent team lets several agents take on one piece of work together, each working its own part and sharing and challenging what the others find, where one session would work through it alone.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>An agent team lets several agents take on one piece of work together, each working its own part and sharing and challenging what the others find, where one session would work through it alone.</p>\n<p>An agent team is several <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">sessions</a> working on one piece of work together. A <strong>team lead</strong> sets the work out, assigns it and brings the results together; <strong>teammates</strong>, each a full, independent session with its own context, take their parts and message each other and the lead directly. Teammates are <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agents</a> in their own right, not <a href=\"/glossary/subagent/\">subagents</a>, since they interact with each other rather than only with the agent that started them. Their messages travel within the team, as <a href=\"/glossary/cross-session-messaging/\">cross-session messaging</a> carries them between sessions outside one.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>Knowledge Islands' guardian, warden and housekeepers work as an agent team in this sense when they take one job together: separate sessions messaging each other, though not through Anthropic's feature.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"/glossary/remote-agent/\">remote agent</a> is an agent in another system, reached over a protocol.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.remote-agent",
      "slug": "remote-agent",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Remote agent",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/remote-agent/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.remote-agent.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A remote agent lets an agent use another system's agent as a service, asking it for work and getting results back without knowing or controlling how that system works.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A remote agent lets an agent use another system's agent as a service, asking it for work and getting results back without knowing or controlling how that system works.</p>\n<p>A remote agent is an <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agent</a>, or a system of agents, in another system, which a client agent reaches over a protocol and which stays opaque to it: its memory, tools and inner agents are hidden. The word and its sense are the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-remote-agent\">Agent2Agent protocol's remote agent</a>, the A2A server. It is not a <a href=\"/glossary/subagent/\">subagent</a>, since it is independent of the agent that asks it, even where a vendor calls it one.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>No Knowledge Islands system offers or reaches a remote agent today; a session on another rig is a peer, in <a href=\"/glossary/cross-session-messaging/\">cross-session messaging</a>'s sense, not a remote agent, because it is in the same system.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"/glossary/tool/\">tool</a> is a function an agent can call to act or fetch something beyond the model.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.tool",
      "slug": "tool",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Tool",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/tool/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.tool.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A tool lets an agent act on the world, or fetch what it does not know, beyond what the model can do with its own text.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A tool lets an agent act on the world, or fetch what it does not know, beyond what the model can do with its own text.</p>\n<p>A tool is a function an <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agent</a> can call, described by a name, a description saying when and how to use it, and an input schema in JSON Schema. The model only asks for the call, choosing the tool from its description; the <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a>, the application, or a server on the agent's behalf runs it and hands the result back, so the model never runs code itself (<a href=\"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview\">A12</a>, <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/function-calling\">O14</a>). The <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-tools\">Model Context Protocol's tools</a> are tools offered by a server in this sense, discovered and called over the protocol.</p>\n<p>A tool is not a skill: a skill is knowledge and procedure the agent reads, and it may tell the agent which tools to call.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>The tools Knowledge Islands sessions call come from the agent harness itself, such as reading and editing files and running commands, and from MCP servers declared in the portable MCP inventory under <code>ki-env-binding</code>.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>An <a href=\"/glossary/instruction-file/\">instruction file</a> holds the standing instructions a harness loads into a session.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.instruction-file",
      "slug": "instruction-file",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Instruction file",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/instruction-file/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.instruction-file.md",
      "summaryHtml": "An instruction file gives every session in a place the same standing instructions, so they need not be repeated in each prompt.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>An instruction file gives every session in a place the same standing instructions, so they need not be repeated in each prompt.</p>\n<p>An instruction file is a plain Markdown file that an <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a> finds by its name and loads into a <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">session</a> as standing context: from the user's own folder for every project, from the repository's root, and from folders nearer the work, the nearer file taking precedence. Harnesses load the user's and the root's files when a session starts, and some load a folder's file only once the agent works there (<a href=\"https://agents.md/\">AG1</a>, <a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory\">A13</a>, <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/agents-md\">O15</a>, <a href=\"https://geminicli.com/docs/cli/gemini-md/\">G10</a>). <code>AGENTS.md</code> is the open, shared name (<a href=\"https://agents.md/\">AG1</a>); Claude Code reads <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, and can read <code>AGENTS.md</code> in its place (<a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory\">A13</a>); Codex reads <code>AGENTS.md</code>, with an <code>AGENTS.override.md</code> beside it (<a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/agents-md\">O15</a>); and Gemini CLI reads <code>GEMINI.md</code>, its name configurable to <code>AGENTS.md</code> (<a href=\"https://geminicli.com/docs/cli/gemini-md/\">G10</a>). The instructions are context the model follows, not configuration the harness enforces (<a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory\">A13</a>).</p>\n<p>An instruction file is not a <a href=\"/glossary/skill/\">skill</a>: it is always loaded, where a skill costs little until a task calls for it.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>A Knowledge Islands repository keeps its shared orientation in a physical root <code>AGENTS.md</code>, with any root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> a thin Claude-only appendix that imports it, as <code>ki-repo</code> sets; the owner's own preferences sit in the user-level files.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"/glossary/plugin/\">plugin</a> is an installable bundle that extends an agent harness with skills and more.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.plugin",
      "slug": "plugin",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Plugin",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/plugin/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.plugin.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A plugin lets a whole working setup for an agent harness be installed, and shared, in one step, rather than gathered a piece at a time.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A plugin lets a whole working setup for an agent harness be installed, and shared, in one step, rather than gathered a piece at a time.</p>\n<p>A plugin is an installable bundle that extends an <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a>. It holds <a href=\"/glossary/skill/\">skills</a> and often hooks, subagent definitions and <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-server\">MCP servers</a>, packaged, installed, enabled and shared as one unit, usually from a catalogue called a marketplace. It is the way skills are distributed, not a skill itself.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>Each territory's <a href=\"/glossary/backpack/\">backpack</a>, such as the KI backpack, is Knowledge Islands' plugin: a bundle of skills, subagent definitions, evals and hooks. It is a close match until the repositories ship plugin manifests.</p>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<p>The industry's terms in reading order end here. The channel's place in the order is still to settle, and the system's own terms follow in a subgraph of their own.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "industry.channel",
      "slug": "channel",
      "kind": "industry",
      "label": "Channel",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/channel/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/industry/industry.channel.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A channel lets something outside an agent's session reach it while it runs, so the agent can react to events that happen while nobody is watching.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A channel lets something outside an agent's session reach it while it runs, so the agent can react to events that happen while nobody is watching.</p>\n<p>Option A of CARD-CHANNEL was taken on the owner's word for every high-confidence recommendation (12 November 2026): the industry's words only. The owner agreed the card's wording on 16 November 2026.</p>\n<h3>What the industry says</h3>\n<p>Anthropic, who lead (LEADERS), define the word: &quot;A channel is an MCP server that pushes events into your running Claude Code session&quot;, which may be two-way, the agent replying through it &quot;like a chat bridge&quot;; Telegram, Discord and iMessage are the first, and channels are a research preview (<a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/channels\">Claude Code channels</a>). Anthropic set it apart from three neighbours on the same page:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>An integration</strong> starts a new session from an outside system, as Claude in Slack does from a mention; a channel reaches the session already open.</li><li><strong>A standard <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">MCP server</a></strong> is queried by the agent; &quot;nothing is pushed to the session&quot;.</li><li><strong>Remote Control</strong> lets you drive your own session from elsewhere, which is a <a href=\"/glossary/client/\">client</a>, not a channel.</li></ul>\n<p>Sessions messaging one another are a fourth thing, which Anthropic call <strong>cross-session messaging</strong>: one session writes a plain-text message to another, found through its &quot;Peer address&quot; (<a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/cross-session-messaging\">cross-session messaging</a>). OpenAI's nearest words are triggers for scheduled tasks and integrations such as Linear and Slack, each starting work rather than reaching a running session. Microsoft's Copilot Studio uses &quot;channel&quot; for where an agent is published, such as Teams or a website, a different sense. The <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-channels\">Agent Host Protocol's channels</a> are a third sense again: a subscription to one resource's state. &quot;Agentic channel&quot; is used only in commerce, for a sales channel served by agents.</p>\n<h3>The decision and the option set aside</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>A, the industry's words only, taken.</strong> This card takes Anthropic's sense: a channel pushes events from outside into a running <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">session</a>. Cards to follow: an integration, which starts a new session, and session-to-session messaging, named from Anthropic's cross-session messaging, all three under a broader card for the ways into a session. Microsoft's and AHP's senses go on their own cards.</li><li><strong>B, our own qualified labels, set aside.</strong> The system would have kept &quot;agentic channel&quot; for every route into or between sessions, in a subgraph of its own, at the cost of a word the industry does not use.</li></ul>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>Knowledge Islands' sessions work together in meetings, each message a file in the meeting's folder in the store, which the participants read rather than have pushed into their sessions, so a meeting is not a channel; sessions on a rig that message each other directly are <a href=\"/glossary/cross-session-messaging/\">cross-session messaging</a>. A notice pushed into a running session from outside, as the Cairn app might push one, would be a channel.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "system.guild",
      "slug": "guild",
      "kind": "system",
      "label": "Guild",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/guild/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/system/system.guild.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A guild lets a session find the <a href=\"/glossary/skill/\">skills</a> it needs among many, by grouping those that belong together and work together under one purpose a reader can name.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A guild lets a session find the <a href=\"/glossary/skill/\">skills</a> it needs among many, by grouping those that belong together and work together under one purpose a reader can name.</p>\n<p>A guild is a set of skills led by one lead skill, named after the guild, which says what the guild is for and discloses its other skills as they are needed, the way a skill discloses its own files. It is not a layer above skills: nothing new sits above a skill, and the industry has no word for it, so it is Knowledge Islands' own. Keystone is the guild of guilds: it holds what every other guild rests on and each guild's lead.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>The skills of a <a href=\"/glossary/backpack/\">backpack</a> each declare their guild in front matter, <code>ki-guild</code>, and a lead the guild it leads, <code>ki-leads</code>. The <code>ki-skills</code> skill's guilds standard sets the rules, and <code>ki-keystone</code> holds the guild map. A guild is kept by its guildmaster, within the backpack its territory carries.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "system.rig",
      "slug": "rig",
      "kind": "system",
      "label": "Rig",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/rig/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/system/system.rig.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A rig lets Knowledge Islands set up a machine, and put it right, the same way every time, so a session knows what it will find there.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A rig lets Knowledge Islands set up a machine, and put it right, the same way every time, so a session knows what it will find there.</p>\n<p>A rig is a <a href=\"/glossary/machine/\">machine</a> Knowledge Islands manages through Rig, its workstation tool, with a Rig profile and a declared setup, such as sol or terra. It is the same idea as a machine, not a narrower one: rig is Knowledge Islands' word where the industry says machine, <code>skos:exactMatch</code>. An agent host runs on a rig, so a rig is never called a host (HOST-SENSES).</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>Each rig has a housekeeper, an agent that keeps the rig's setup, as Hob Sol keeps sol and Hob Terra keeps terra. An <a href=\"/glossary/actor/\">actor's</a> roster file names the rig it runs on. Rig, the tool, is written with a capital; a rig, the machine, without.</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "system.actor",
      "slug": "actor",
      "kind": "system",
      "label": "Actor",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/actor/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/system/system.actor.md",
      "summaryHtml": "An actor gives each piece of work someone who answers for it, who stays the same while the sessions that act for it come and go.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>An actor gives each piece of work someone who answers for it, who stays the same while the sessions that act for it come and go.</p>\n<p>An actor is whoever acts at a node of the work tree, a person or an <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agent</a>, the owner among them. An actor persists across <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">sessions</a> and agent hosts, where a session is one run: many sessions may act as one actor, one after another or at once. The industry has no word for it; the Unified Modeling Language's actor, someone outside a system who uses it, is related but not the same, and is not mapped.</p>\n<p>An agent actor runs as one of two types (ACTOR-TYPES): a <strong>session actor</strong>, run as an agent host's session, opened by an opener and a bootstrap and kept by hand, or a <strong>bot actor</strong>, run as a <a href=\"/glossary/bot/\">bot</a>, a named identity its agent host keeps and wakes.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>One roster file each:</strong> every actor has one file in the store's roster, named by its id. It opens <code>ki-id: actor:&lt;id&gt;</code>, then <code>ki-document-type: actor</code>, an optional human <code>ki-name</code>, then <code>ki-kind</code>, the actor's type: <code>agent</code> for a session actor, <code>bot</code> for a bot actor, or <code>character</code> for one that never runs, as <code>ki-id: actor:wayland</code> with <code>ki-name: Wayland</code> and <code>ki-kind: bot</code> (ACTOR-KEY and ACTOR-TYPE-FIELD, in the store contract's <code>ki-</code> fields).</li><li><strong>An actor with no name of its own</strong> takes the id of its role in its <a href=\"/glossary/work-package/\">work package</a>, <code>&lt;package&gt;@&lt;role&gt;</code>, as <code>actor:ki.cairn@information-architect</code>, its roster file named the same (SEAT-AT).</li><li><strong>Ids, never copies:</strong> a work package's <code>workers</code> entry points at its actor by <code>id: actor:&lt;id&gt;</code>, and a meeting's participant carries only its actor id and how it is reached, so no file repeats another's fields.</li><li><strong>Presence lives in the roster file:</strong> an agent actor's <a href=\"/glossary/agent-host/\">agent host</a> and <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a>, as <code>ki-host</code> and <code>ki-harness</code> (PRESENCE-FIELDS a), and the <a href=\"/glossary/rig/\">rig</a> it runs on are its roster file's; who acts where is the work tree's.</li></ul>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "system.work-package",
      "slug": "work-package",
      "kind": "system",
      "label": "Work package",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/work-package/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/system/system.work-package.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A work package gives one piece of work a single place where its scope, its changes and where they stand are kept, so whoever picks it up knows where things are.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A work package gives one piece of work a single place where its scope, its changes and where they stand are kept, so whoever picks it up knows where things are.</p>\n<p>A work package is the folder for one piece of work above a work item, holding its entry file, its change documents and its sub-packages: a node of the work tree. Outside the store it is always written in full, work package, since package alone means too many things. The industry has no word for it, and none is mapped: the project-management work package, the lowest level of a work breakdown, is a different sense.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>A node's id</strong> is <code>&lt;territory&gt;.&lt;slug&gt;</code>, with a further dot segment for a node beneath a node, as <code>ki.cairn</code> or <code>personal.rig.sol</code>; each territory's top is <code>&lt;territory&gt;.summit</code>, beneath the one root (NODE-ID).</li><li><strong>A sub-package</strong> is a strand of a package that its own <a href=\"/glossary/actor/\">actor</a> works, <code>&lt;parent id&gt;+&lt;slug&gt;</code>, as <code>hnr.summit+website</code>, in the folder <code>work/hnr/hnr.summit/website/</code>; a strand that needs an actor of its own is a node beneath instead (SUMMIT-SUB-PACKAGES).</li><li><strong>An actor's place in a package</strong> is written <code>&lt;package&gt;@&lt;role&gt;</code>, as <code>ki.cairn@warden</code> or <code>hnr.summit+website@information-architect</code>: <code>.</code> builds a package's name, <code>+</code> adds a strand and <code>@</code> names the place (SEAT-AT).</li><li><strong>Its shape:</strong> the <code>ki-work-package</code> skill sets the entry file, <code>_index.md</code>, and what a package holds; the change documents follow <code>ki-authoring</code>'s change format.</li></ul>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "system.backpack",
      "slug": "backpack",
      "kind": "system",
      "label": "Backpack",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/backpack/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/system/system.backpack.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A backpack lets a territory carry everything its agents need to work its way, and install it on any rig in one step.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A backpack lets a territory carry everything its agents need to work its way, and install it on any rig in one step.</p>\n<p>A backpack is a territory's installable bundle of <a href=\"/glossary/skill/\">skills</a>, subagent definitions, evals and hooks, such as the KI backpack: Knowledge Islands' <a href=\"/glossary/plugin/\">plugin</a>. It maps onto plugin, <code>skos:closeMatch</code>, and will match it exactly once its repositories ship plugin manifests. It is written as a kind, in lower case: the KI backpack, each territory's backpack.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<p>Each territory's backpack is a repository named <code>&lt;territory&gt;-agent-backpack</code>, as <code>ki-agent-backpack</code>, and its skills are grouped in <a href=\"/glossary/guild/\">guilds</a>. A backpack is not an <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a>: it runs no loop and calls no model, so agent harness keeps the industry's sense (HARNESS-NAME, BACKPACK-DEPTH).</p>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "system.role",
      "slug": "role",
      "kind": "system",
      "label": "Role",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/role/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/system/system.role.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A role says what a job owns and what it never does, so whoever takes a place in a piece of work knows its bounds before acting.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A role says what a job owns and what it never does, so whoever takes a place in a piece of work knows its bounds before acting.</p>\n<p>A role is a job within a tier, leader, manager or maker, from the role catalogue, such as governor, warden or housekeeper. It is the job an <a href=\"/glossary/actor/\">actor</a> does in a <a href=\"/glossary/work-package/\">work package</a>, and the same role may be held in many packages by different actors. It closely matches the role of agent frameworks, such as CrewAI's agent <code>role</code>, &quot;the agent's function and expertise within the crew&quot; (<a href=\"https://docs.crewai.com/en/concepts/agents\">CR1</a>), though a role here is a shared catalogue entry with its bounds, not a free-text field of one agent.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>One catalogue:</strong> <code>ki-work-package</code>'s roles standard holds every role, its tier, glyph, what it owns and what it never does; nothing else decides a role.</li><li><strong>The job after the <code>@</code>:</strong> an actor's place in a package, its <a href=\"/glossary/seat/\">seat</a>, is written <code>&lt;package&gt;@&lt;role&gt;</code>, as <code>ki.cairn@warden</code>, so the role is the part after the <code>@</code> (SEAT-AT).</li><li><strong>One file each in the store:</strong> each role has a file in the store's roster, <code>roster/roles/&lt;role&gt;.md</code>, opening <code>ki-id: role:&lt;role&gt;</code>, its fields taken word for word from the catalogue (ROLES-IN-STORE).</li></ul>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "system.seat",
      "slug": "seat",
      "kind": "system",
      "label": "Seat",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/glossary/seat/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/system/system.seat.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A seat gives each job in a piece of work an address of its own, so work can be routed to it whoever holds it.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A seat gives each job in a piece of work an address of its own, so work can be routed to it whoever holds it.</p>\n<p>A seat is the place for one <a href=\"/glossary/role/\">role</a> in one <a href=\"/glossary/work-package/\">work package</a>. An <a href=\"/glossary/actor/\">actor</a> holds it, and the same actor may hold seats in many packages; when the actor changes, the seat and its address stay. The industry has no word for it: agent frameworks give a role to an agent, not a place in a piece of work, so it is not mapped.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>The address:</strong> a seat is written <code>&lt;package&gt;@&lt;role&gt;</code>, or <code>&lt;package&gt;+&lt;slug&gt;@&lt;role&gt;</code> in a sub-package, as <code>ki.cairn@warden</code> and <code>hnr.summit+website@information-architect</code>: <code>.</code> builds a package's name, <code>+</code> adds a strand and <code>@</code> names a seat (SEAT-AT).</li><li><strong>The binding:</strong> a package's live seats are its <code>ki-workers</code> entries, each a role, its tier and at most the holding actor's id; a seat that has ended leaves the list, and which seats an actor holds is read from there, never stored in the roster.</li><li><strong>A seat's own actor:</strong> an actor with no name of its own takes its seat's id, as <code>actor:ki.cairn@information-architect</code>, its roster file named the same; it may be given a name later in that file, as the actor at <code>cairn.summit@voice</code> is Natter.</li></ul>",
      "instances": []
    },
    {
      "id": "system.bot",
      "slug": "bot",
      "kind": "system",
      "label": "Bot",
      "status": "proposal",
      "url": "/glossary/bot/",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/system/system.bot.md",
      "summaryHtml": "A bot gives an <a href=\"/glossary/actor/\">actor</a> a home of its own on an <a href=\"/glossary/agent-host/\">agent host</a>, so it can be reached by name and woken by messages and schedules without anyone opening a session for it, while the work it does stays on the record every actor shares.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>A bot gives an <a href=\"/glossary/actor/\">actor</a> a home of its own on an <a href=\"/glossary/agent-host/\">agent host</a>, so it can be reached by name and woken by messages and schedules without anyone opening a session for it, while the work it does stays on the record every actor shares.</p>\n<p>A bot is an <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agent</a> that has its own memory and processes (BOT-DEFINITION): a durable, named identity on one agent host, with a folder of its own, mail addressed to its name, and scheduled or event-driven runs, that acts through many <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">sessions</a> over time. It is one of the two types of actor that run. A <strong>session actor</strong> is run as an agent host's session, opened by an opener and a bootstrap and kept by hand; a <strong>bot actor</strong> is run as a bot, its host keeping it and waking it (ACTOR-TYPES). The industry uses &quot;bot&quot; loosely, for any chat or automation program, and has no agreed term for an agent with its own durable identity and processes; agent hosts that offer one name it themselves, as Orca's bots are. So it is Knowledge Islands' own word, and it is not mapped.</p>\n<h3>Our conventions</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>The roster records the type:</strong> an actor's <code>ki-kind</code> is <code>agent</code> for a session actor and <code>bot</code> for a bot actor, and <code>ki-name</code> names both alike; a bot keeps <code>ki-host</code> for its agent host and <code>ki-rig</code> for its <a href=\"/glossary/rig/\">rig</a>, and its host reaches it by its roster id, so there is no bot field of its own (ACTOR-TYPE-FIELD).</li><li><strong>The same rules as a session:</strong> a bot works under the same rules, access and remit as a session actor in its seat, and a peer's message is never the owner's approval. Its routine wakes for its own duties are its to set up; a new kind of automation beyond them gets a one-line mention to the owner first. There is no fallback session: the bot replaces the session, and the owner can restart a session from the <a href=\"/glossary/work-package/\">work package</a> at any time. No Family or legal bots until the trial goes well (BOT-CONSTRAINTS a).</li><li><strong>The store is the record:</strong> the work package, its change documents, meetings and records hold what lasts, in forms any host can read; the bot's own task list and memory are working aids, never the record (BOTS-STANDARDS, BOT-CONSTRAINTS a).</li><li><strong>Its own files are in the roster:</strong> who and what an actor is, its soul, lives in its roster folder in Git, <code>SOUL.md</code> beside its entry; a bot's own files on its host only point there (BOT-RECORD, ROSTER-SOUL).</li><li><strong>How a bot works:</strong> the <code>ki-agentic-bots</code> skill sets the practice a bot actor follows on its host: its bootstrap on every wake, its background work, its mail, its memory and its questions.</li></ul>",
      "instances": []
    }
  ],
  "specifications": [
    {
      "id": "spec.agent-skills",
      "anchor": "spec-agent-skills",
      "label": "Agent Skills specification",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/specifications/#spec-agent-skills",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/agent-skills/spec.agent-skills.md",
      "summaryHtml": "The Agent Skills specification lets one <a href=\"/glossary/skill/\">skill</a> work in any agent harness that adopts it, by fixing the form a skill takes (<a href=\"https://agentskills.io/specification\">specification</a>).",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>The Agent Skills specification lets one <a href=\"/glossary/skill/\">skill</a> work in any agent harness that adopts it, by fixing the form a skill takes (<a href=\"https://agentskills.io/specification\">specification</a>).</p>\n<p>A skill is a directory holding at least a <code>SKILL.md</code> file. Its front matter carries a <code>name</code>, of up to 64 characters, and a <code>description</code>, of up to 1,024, and may carry <code>license</code>, <code>compatibility</code>, <code>metadata</code> and <code>allowed-tools</code>; the body holds the instructions. The directory may also hold <code>scripts/</code>, <code>references/</code> and <code>assets/</code>, and any other files or directories. Skills are loaded by <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-agent-skills-progressive-disclosure\">progressive disclosure</a>.</p>\n<p>The specification advises keeping file references one level deep from <code>SKILL.md</code>. That concerns chains of references, one file sending the reader to another, not the depth of folders, which the specification leaves open.</p>\n<p>Anthropic, OpenAI, Google and Microsoft each build their skills on it.</p>",
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "spec.agent-skills.progressive-disclosure",
          "anchor": "spec-agent-skills-progressive-disclosure",
          "label": "Progressive disclosure, in the Agent Skills specification",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-agent-skills-progressive-disclosure",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/agent-skills/spec.agent-skills.progressive-disclosure.md",
          "summaryHtml": "Progressive disclosure keeps a <a href=\"/glossary/skill/\">skill</a> cheap until it is needed, so an agent can hold many skills at once. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-agent-skills\">Agent Skills specification</a> (<a href=\"https://agentskills.io/specification\">specification</a>).",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>Progressive disclosure keeps a <a href=\"/glossary/skill/\">skill</a> cheap until it is needed, so an agent can hold many skills at once. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-agent-skills\">Agent Skills specification</a> (<a href=\"https://agentskills.io/specification\">specification</a>).</p>\n<p>A skill is loaded in three levels:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Metadata:</strong> the <code>name</code> and <code>description</code>, about 100 tokens, loaded at startup for every skill.</li><li><strong>Instructions:</strong> the body of <code>SKILL.md</code>, advised to stay under 5,000 tokens, loaded when the skill is activated.</li><li><strong>Resources:</strong> the files the skill bundles, loaded only as they are needed.</li></ol>"
        }
      ],
      "conforming": [
