    {
      "id": "spec.mcp",
      "anchor": "spec-mcp",
      "label": "Model Context Protocol (MCP)",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/specifications/#spec-mcp",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/mcp/spec.mcp.md",
      "summaryHtml": "The Model Context Protocol gives any LLM application one standard way to reach the context, data and tools it needs, so one integration works in every application that adopts it (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28\">MCP1</a>).",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>The Model Context Protocol gives any LLM application one standard way to reach the context, data and tools it needs, so one integration works in every application that adopts it (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28\">MCP1</a>).</p>\n<p>MCP is a protocol of JSON-RPC 2.0 messages between three roles: an <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-host\">MCP host</a>, the application, which creates one <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-client\">MCP client</a> for each <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-server\">MCP server</a> it connects to. A server offers resources, prompts and <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-tools\">tools</a>; a client may offer elicitation, asking the user for more information. Messages travel over its <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-transports\">transports</a>. Optional extensions, among them tasks and Skills over MCP, are negotiated on top (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28\">MCP1</a>).</p>\n<p>The current revision is 2026-07-28 (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/versioning\">MCP7</a>). It made MCP stateless: the <code>initialize</code> handshake and the protocol-level session of revisions up to 2025-11-25 are gone, and every request carries its own protocol version and capabilities (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/changelog\">MCP8</a>). MCP has no session in this graph's sense. The same revision deprecates four features, which still work but which new implementations should not adopt: roots, sampling, logging, and dynamic client registration, replaced by Client ID Metadata Documents (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/changelog\">MCP8</a>).</p>\n<p>An <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a> uses MCP to reach its tools; the <a href=\"/glossary/tool/\">tool</a> card rests on it. The <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp\">Agent Client Protocol</a> works beside it.</p>\n<p>MCP sits in the Linux Foundation's Agentic AI Foundation (AAIF), to which Anthropic gave it on 9 December 2025; its maintainers keep its governance (<a href=\"https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2025-12-09-mcp-joins-agentic-ai-foundation/\">MCP11</a>).</p>",
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "spec.mcp.host",
          "anchor": "spec-mcp-host",
          "label": "MCP host, in the Model Context Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-mcp-host",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/mcp/spec.mcp.host.md",
          "summaryHtml": "An MCP host keeps the conversation and the user's consent in one place, so that each server it uses sees only what it needs. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">Model Context Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/architecture\">MCP2</a>).",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>An MCP host keeps the conversation and the user's consent in one place, so that each server it uses sees only what it needs. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">Model Context Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/architecture\">MCP2</a>).</p>\n<p>MCP defines its hosts as &quot;LLM applications that initiate connections&quot; (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28\">MCP1</a>). The MCP host creates and manages one <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-client\">MCP client</a> for each server, controls their permissions and lifecycle, enforces security policies and consent, coordinates the model, and aggregates context across clients. The full conversation stays with it (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/architecture\">MCP2</a>).</p>\n<p>This is a third sense of &quot;host&quot;, and it is never written bare. Its role is closest to an <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a>, which holds the tools and the context and talks to the model. It is not an <a href=\"/glossary/agent-host/\">agent host</a>, which runs harnesses and owns their sessions for clients to display, although one program can be both. MCP's hosts also include LLM applications that are not agentic, such as chat interfaces.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.mcp.client",
          "anchor": "spec-mcp-client",
          "label": "MCP client, in the Model Context Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-mcp-client",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/mcp/spec.mcp.client.md",
          "summaryHtml": "An MCP client keeps each server apart from the others, so that one server can never see into another. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">Model Context Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/architecture\">MCP2</a>).",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>An MCP client keeps each server apart from the others, so that one server can never see into another. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">Model Context Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/architecture\">MCP2</a>).</p>\n<p>MCP calls its clients &quot;connectors within the host application&quot; (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28\">MCP1</a>). The <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-host\">MCP host</a> creates one MCP client for each <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-server\">MCP server</a>, and each talks to exactly that one server. It attaches the protocol version and its capabilities to every request, routes messages both ways, manages subscriptions and notifications, and keeps the security boundary between servers (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/architecture\">MCP2</a>). It may offer the server elicitation, asking the user for more information (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28\">MCP1</a>).</p>\n<p>An MCP client is not a <a href=\"/glossary/client/\">client</a> in this graph's sense: it is plumbing inside an application, not a window onto a session. Most agents that speak the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp\">Agent Client Protocol</a> are MCP clients as well (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/related-protocols\">ACP5</a>).</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.mcp.server",
          "anchor": "spec-mcp-server",
          "label": "MCP server, in the Model Context Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-mcp-server",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/mcp/spec.mcp.server.md",
          "summaryHtml": "An MCP server packages one focused capability, such as a database or an external service, so that any application speaking MCP can use it. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">Model Context Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/architecture\">MCP2</a>).",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>An MCP server packages one focused capability, such as a database or an external service, so that any application speaking MCP can use it. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">Model Context Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/architecture\">MCP2</a>).</p>\n<p>MCP defines servers as &quot;services that provide context and capabilities&quot; (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28\">MCP1</a>), local processes or remote services. Each offers any of three features to an <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-client\">MCP client</a>:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Resources:</strong> context and data, such as files or database schemas, each named by a URI, for the application to include as it chooses (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/resources\">MCP9</a>).</li><li><strong>Prompts:</strong> templated messages the user selects, often as slash commands (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/prompts\">MCP10</a>).</li><li><strong><a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-tools\">Tools</a>:</strong> functions the model can call.</li></ol>\n<p>A server should be easy to build and to combine with others. It never reads the whole conversation or sees into another server; where it needs input from the user, it asks for it in its reply, by elicitation (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/architecture\">MCP2</a>, <a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/changelog\">MCP8</a>). Sampling, by which a server asked for the model's help, is deprecated in the 2026-07-28 revision (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/changelog\">MCP8</a>).</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.mcp.tools",
          "anchor": "spec-mcp-tools",
          "label": "Tools, in the Model Context Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-mcp-tools",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/mcp/spec.mcp.tools.md",
          "summaryHtml": "MCP's tools let a model act on systems outside itself, such as querying a database or calling an API, through any <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-server\">MCP server</a> that offers them. They are part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">Model Context Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools\">MCP3</a>).",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>MCP's tools let a model act on systems outside itself, such as querying a database or calling an API, through any <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-server\">MCP server</a> that offers them. They are part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">Model Context Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools\">MCP3</a>).</p>\n<p>A tool is named by a unique <code>name</code> and described by a <code>description</code>, an <code>inputSchema</code> in JSON Schema, and optionally a <code>title</code>, an <code>outputSchema</code> and <code>annotations</code>. A client discovers tools with <code>tools/list</code> and calls one with <code>tools/call</code>; the result carries text, images, audio, resource links, embedded resources or structured content (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools\">MCP3</a>).</p>\n<p>Tools are model-controlled: the model chooses them from its context and the user's prompts. In this graph's terms, the <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a> makes the call, through its MCP client, and the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-server\">MCP server</a> runs the tool and returns the result (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools\">MCP3</a>). The specification says there should always be a human able to deny a call, and treats a tool's annotations as untrusted unless its server is trusted (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools\">MCP3</a>).</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/glossary/tool/\">tool</a> card rests on this part.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.mcp.transports",
          "anchor": "spec-mcp-transports",
          "label": "Transports, in the Model Context Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-mcp-transports",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/mcp/spec.mcp.transports.md",
          "summaryHtml": "MCP's transports let the same messages reach a server on the same machine or across the internet, with the same meaning on every one. They are part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">Model Context Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/transports\">MCP4</a>).",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>MCP's transports let the same messages reach a server on the same machine or across the internet, with the same meaning on every one. They are part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">Model Context Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/transports\">MCP4</a>).</p>\n<p>A transport says only how messages are framed and delivered, how request metadata travels, and how a request is cancelled. MCP defines two:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>stdio:</strong> the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-client\">MCP client</a> launches the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp-server\">MCP server</a> as a subprocess and they exchange newline-delimited JSON-RPC over its standard input and output (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/transports/stdio\">MCP5</a>).</li><li><strong>Streamable HTTP:</strong> the server runs as an independent process with a single endpoint; each message is an HTTP POST, answered with one JSON object or a stream of Server-Sent Events for that request (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/transports/streamable-http\">MCP6</a>).</li></ol>\n<p>Clients and servers may add custom transports that keep the same messages. Streamable HTTP replaced the HTTP+SSE transport of 2024-11-05 in revision 2025-03-26, which deprecated it; revision 2026-07-28 marks it Deprecated under MCP's feature lifecycle, and removes Streamable HTTP's session id (<a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/transports/streamable-http\">MCP6</a>, <a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/changelog\">MCP8</a>).</p>"
        }
      ],
