    {
      "id": "spec.acp",
      "anchor": "spec-acp",
      "label": "Agent Client Protocol (ACP)",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/specifications/#spec-acp",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/acp/spec.acp.md",
      "summaryHtml": "The Agent Client Protocol lets any code editor drive any coding agent, so that an agent works in every editor that adopts it and each side can develop on its own (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/introduction\">ACP1</a>).",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>The Agent Client Protocol lets any code editor drive any coding agent, so that an agent works in every editor that adopts it and each side can develop on its own (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/introduction\">ACP1</a>).</p>\n<p>ACP is a protocol of JSON-RPC 2.0 messages between an <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp-client\">ACP client</a>, usually an editor, and an <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp-agent\">ACP agent</a>. The client initialises the connection, opens an <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp-session\">ACP session</a> and sends prompts; the agent streams back updates, reports tool calls and asks permission before acting (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/overview\">ACP2</a>). It reuses the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">Model Context Protocol</a>'s JSON representations where it can, and the client hands the agent the user's MCP servers (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/related-protocols\">ACP5</a>).</p>\n<p>Its one transport is stdio: the client launches the agent as a subprocess and they exchange newline-delimited JSON-RPC. A remote transport over Streamable HTTP is still being specified (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/transports\">ACP4</a>).</p>\n<p>The stable protocol is version 1. Its release of 18 November 2026 carried schema v1.33.0, the draft version 2's schema v2.0.0-alpha.17 and the Rust crate v1.19.0 (<a href=\"https://github.com/agentclientprotocol/agent-client-protocol/releases\">ACP8</a>); ACP releases several times a week, so a card cites a release by its date. Version 2 has been in draft since 20 July 2026 (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/announcements/acp-v2-draft\">ACP7</a>). Zed Industries and JetBrains govern it jointly, under an interim model, while working towards an independent foundation (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/community/governance\">ACP6</a>), and <a href=\"https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/vendors/zed/zed.zed.md\">Zed</a> is one of its clients (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/agents\">agents</a>).</p>\n<p>It is not the Agent Communication Protocol, IBM's BeeAI project, also called ACP, which merged into <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a\">Agent2Agent</a> in 2025 (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/related-protocols\">ACP5</a>).</p>",
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "spec.acp.client",
          "anchor": "spec-acp-client",
          "label": "ACP client, in the Agent Client Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-acp-client",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/acp/spec.acp.client.md",
          "summaryHtml": "An ACP client lets you drive a coding agent from the tool you already work in, usually your editor. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp\">Agent Client Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/overview\">ACP2</a>).",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>An ACP client lets you drive a coding agent from the tool you already work in, usually your editor. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp\">Agent Client Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/overview\">ACP2</a>).</p>\n<p>ACP says clients &quot;provide the interface between users and agents&quot;: typically code editors, though other interfaces qualify. A client launches the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp-agent\">ACP agent</a>, sends prompts, shows the updates and tool calls it streams back, and answers its requests for permission. It may also lend the agent its files and terminals, and passes it the user's MCP servers (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/overview\">ACP2</a>, <a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/architecture\">ACP10</a>). An agent can itself be an ACP client, running other coding agents as subagents (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/related-protocols\">ACP5</a>).</p>\n<p>It is narrower than a <a href=\"/glossary/client/\">client</a> in this graph's sense: it reaches an agent only through ACP. It also does more than a window does, since it starts the agent and serves its files and terminals. <a href=\"https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/vendors/zed/zed.zed.md\">Zed</a> is one (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/agents\">agents</a>).</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.acp.agent",
          "anchor": "spec-acp-agent",
          "label": "ACP agent, in the Agent Client Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-acp-agent",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/acp/spec.acp.agent.md",
          "summaryHtml": "An ACP agent lets one coding agent work in every editor that speaks ACP, without an integration for each. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp\">Agent Client Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/introduction\">ACP1</a>).",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>An ACP agent lets one coding agent work in every editor that speaks ACP, without an integration for each. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp\">Agent Client Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/introduction\">ACP1</a>).</p>\n<p>ACP defines agents as &quot;programs that use generative AI to autonomously modify code&quot;, which usually run as subprocesses of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp-client\">ACP client</a>. An agent negotiates the protocol version, creates and keeps <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp-session\">ACP sessions</a>, runs each prompt and streams its progress back, and asks the client's permission before a tool call (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/overview\">ACP2</a>). Most ACP agents are also MCP clients (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/related-protocols\">ACP5</a>).</p>\n<p>An ACP agent is an <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agent</a> narrowed to coding. It is usually not an <a href=\"/glossary/agent-host/\">agent host</a>: the client launches it as a subprocess and ends it (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/transports\">ACP4</a>), so its sessions do not live independently of their client. Examples include Claude Agent, through Zed's adapter, Codex CLI, through ACP's adapter, and Gemini CLI (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/agents\">ACP9</a>).</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.acp.session",
          "anchor": "spec-acp-session",
          "label": "Session, in the Agent Client Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-acp-session",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/acp/spec.acp.session.md",
          "summaryHtml": "An ACP session keeps one conversation with an agent together, so that one agent can run several at once and a client can come back to any of them. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp\">Agent Client Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/session-setup\">ACP3</a>).",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>An ACP session keeps one conversation with an agent together, so that one agent can run several at once and a client can come back to any of them. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp\">Agent Client Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/session-setup\">ACP3</a>).</p>\n<p>ACP calls a session &quot;a specific conversation or thread&quot; between client and agent, with its own context, conversation history and state. The client opens one with <code>session/new</code>, giving an absolute working directory and the MCP servers to connect to, and the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp-agent\">ACP agent</a> answers with a unique session id. Where the agent supports them, the client can load a session, replaying its history; resume it without replay; list, close or delete sessions (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/session-list\">listing</a>, <a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/session-delete\">deleting</a>). Loading lets a session persist across restarts and be shared between client instances (<a href=\"https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/session-setup\">ACP3</a>).</p>\n<p>It closely matches a <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">session</a> in this graph's sense, but where ACP says &quot;thread&quot;, this graph means a part of a session, never another word for one.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "conforming": [
