    {
      "id": "spec.ahp",
      "anchor": "spec-ahp",
      "label": "Agent Host Protocol (AHP)",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/specifications/#spec-ahp",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/ahp/spec.ahp.md",
      "summaryHtml": "The Agent Host Protocol lets several clients share the same agent sessions, each seeing the same live state, whatever agent runs behind them (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/what-is-ahp.html\">what is AHP</a>).",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>The Agent Host Protocol lets several clients share the same agent sessions, each seeing the same live state, whatever agent runs behind them (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/what-is-ahp.html\">what is AHP</a>).</p>\n<p>It defines how an <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-agent-host\">agent host</a>, which it calls a portable, standalone sessions server, talks to its <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-client\">clients</a>. The agent host holds the authoritative state of each <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-session\">session and its chats</a>; clients subscribe to it through <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-channels\">channels</a> and change it only by actions the agent host puts in order. Messages are JSON-RPC 2.0 over any reliable, ordered, two-way transport, most often WebSocket, and versions are negotiated when a client connects (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/specification/overview.html\">overview</a>, <a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/specification/transport.html\">transport</a>).</p>\n<p>AHP fixes how state is shared, not how an agent works. Below the agent host an agent is reached through its own interface, such as the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp\">Agent Client Protocol</a>: AHP calls itself the coordination layer and ACP the communication layer (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/ahp-and-acp.html\">AHP and ACP</a>). Agents talking to each other are outside it.</p>\n<p>Microsoft publishes it. Specification 1.1.0, released on 13 November 2026, is the latest, and 1.2.0 is in draft (<a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/agent-host-protocol/releases/tag/spec%2Fv1.1.0\">release</a>, <a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/specification/versioning.html\">versioning</a>); its Root, Session, Chat, Terminal, Telemetry and Resource watch channels are Stable, the others not yet (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/specification/versioning.html\">versioning</a>). VS Code's agent host is the reference server (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/implementations.html\">implementations</a>).</p>",
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "spec.ahp.agent-host",
          "anchor": "spec-ahp-agent-host",
          "label": "Agent host, in the Agent Host Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-ahp-agent-host",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/ahp/spec.ahp.agent-host.md",
          "summaryHtml": "The agent host lets sessions live apart from the windows that show them: it owns them, so clients can come and go. It is the server's role in the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp\">Agent Host Protocol</a>, and the source of the <a href=\"/glossary/agent-host/\">agent host</a> term.",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>The agent host lets sessions live apart from the windows that show them: it owns them, so clients can come and go. It is the server's role in the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp\">Agent Host Protocol</a>, and the source of the <a href=\"/glossary/agent-host/\">agent host</a> term.</p>\n<p>AHP describes it as a portable, standalone sessions server (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/what-is-ahp.html\">what is AHP</a>); its pages call it the server, the host and the agent host alike. It owns the authoritative state of every channel that has one, puts every change in order and echoes it to each subscribed client, and where clients conflict it sequences the outcome (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/doctrine.html\">doctrine</a>). It manages sessions, which clients subscribe to by URI, and delegates the agent work to the agents behind it (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/ahp-and-acp.html\">AHP and ACP</a>); its root channel lists the agents it offers (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/specification/subscriptions.html\">channels and subscriptions</a>).</p>\n<p>It wraps an <a href=\"/glossary/agent-harness/\">agent harness</a>: its job is &quot;to normalize whatever the harness exposes into AHP state&quot; (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/mcp.html\">MCP servers</a>). Towards its agents it may speak another protocol, such as the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp\">Agent Client Protocol</a>, translating between the two (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/ahp-and-acp.html\">AHP and ACP</a>).</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.ahp.client",
          "anchor": "spec-ahp-client",
          "label": "Client, in the Agent Host Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-ahp-client",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/ahp/spec.ahp.client.md",
          "summaryHtml": "An AHP client lets a person see and drive sessions an agent host owns, from several places at once. It is a role in the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp\">Agent Host Protocol</a>, in the <a href=\"/glossary/client/\">client</a> term's sense.",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>An AHP client lets a person see and drive sessions an agent host owns, from several places at once. It is a role in the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp\">Agent Host Protocol</a>, in the <a href=\"/glossary/client/\">client</a> term's sense.</p>\n<p>A client connects to an <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-agent-host\">agent host</a>, agrees a protocol version, and subscribes to the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-channels\">channels</a> it wants to follow, receiving a snapshot of each and then every change in order. It sends commands and dispatches actions, applying its own at once and reconciling when the agent host echoes them back among other clients' (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/what-is-ahp.html\">what is AHP</a>). After a dropped connection it reconnects and replays what it missed.</p>\n<p>Any number of clients may share one session. Any of them may cancel a running turn, and any may approve a pending tool call, where the first approval wins and later ones are rejected (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/ahp-and-acp.html\">AHP and ACP</a>).</p>\n<p>AHP names IDEs, web apps and CLIs as clients (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/what-is-ahp.html\">what is AHP</a>); VS Code, AHPX and ahpc are among them, with official client libraries in Rust, TypeScript, Kotlin, Swift and Go (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/implementations.html\">implementations</a>).</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.ahp.session",
          "anchor": "spec-ahp-session",
          "label": "Session and chat, in the Agent Host Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-ahp-session",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/ahp/spec.ahp.session.md",
          "summaryHtml": "AHP's sessions and chats let clients follow and share a piece of agent work, or just one conversation within it. They are part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp\">Agent Host Protocol</a>.",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>AHP's sessions and chats let clients follow and share a piece of agent work, or just one conversation within it. They are part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp\">Agent Host Protocol</a>.</p>\n<p>A session is the coordination scope for one or more chats. Its state holds its lifecycle, title and status, the agent that backs it, its working directory, customizations, changesets, active clients and the catalogue of its chats (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/specification/session-channel.html\">session channel</a>). A client chooses the session's id when it asks the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-agent-host\">agent host</a> to create it, and the agent host then starts the agent.</p>\n<p>A chat carries one conversation: its turns, streamed responses, tool calls, pending messages and requests for input. Every chat belongs to a session and records how it began: made by the user; forked from another chat at a completed turn; opened as a side chat, a conversation of its own using another chat's context; or spawned by a tool call in another chat, as when work is delegated to a subagent (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/specification/chat-channel.html\">chat channel</a>). Only one turn runs at a time in each chat (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/ahp-and-acp.html\">AHP and ACP</a>).</p>\n<p>AHP's session is close to the <a href=\"/glossary/session/\">session</a> term, but its chats are not all that card's threads. Only a chat spawned by a tool call is one: a <a href=\"/glossary/subagent/\">subagent</a>'s thread. A chat the user makes, a fork or a side chat is a conversation of its own, nearer an <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-acp-session\">ACP session</a> than a thread.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.ahp.channels",
          "anchor": "spec-ahp-channels",
          "label": "Channels and subscriptions, in the Agent Host Protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-ahp-channels",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/ahp/spec.ahp.channels.md",
          "summaryHtml": "Channels keep every client of an agent host in step, so each sees the same state and can recover it after a break. They are the core of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp\">Agent Host Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/what-is-ahp.html\">what is AHP</a>).",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>Channels keep every client of an agent host in step, so each sees the same state and can recover it after a break. They are the core of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp\">Agent Host Protocol</a> (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/guide/what-is-ahp.html\">what is AHP</a>).</p>\n<p>AHP defines a channel as &quot;a sequence of messages that is identified by a URI&quot;, and every channel has a state, empty for some, as the telemetry channel's, <code>ahp-otlp:</code>, always is. Among them are the root, <code>ahp-root://</code>, and each session, chat, terminal and changeset (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/specification/subscriptions.html\">channels and subscriptions</a>). A <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-client\">client</a> subscribes to a channel and receives a snapshot of its state, which the agent host may omit where the state is empty, then the actions that change it, numbered in order by the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-ahp-agent-host\">agent host</a>, and notifications for events the state does not keep. Every request and notification names its channel, so it can be routed by method and channel alone. The root channel's state holds the agent host's agents, terminals and configuration; sessions are listed by a request, <code>listSessions</code> (<a href=\"https://microsoft.github.io/agent-host-protocol/specification/subscriptions.html\">channels and subscriptions</a>).</p>\n<p>An AHP channel carries the state of one resource to its subscribers. It is not a <a href=\"/glossary/channel/\">channel</a> in this graph's sense, something outside a session pushing events into it while it runs; that card names AHP's as a sense of its own.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "conforming": []
    },
    {
      "id": "spec.a2a",
      "anchor": "spec-a2a",
      "label": "Agent2Agent protocol (A2A)",
      "status": "agreed",
      "url": "/specifications/#spec-a2a",
      "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/a2a/spec.a2a.md",
      "summaryHtml": "The Agent2Agent protocol lets independent agents, built by different vendors on different frameworks, find each other and work together without access to each other's internal state, memory or tools (<a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/\">specification</a>).",
      "bodyHtml": "<p>The Agent2Agent protocol lets independent agents, built by different vendors on different frameworks, find each other and work together without access to each other's internal state, memory or tools (<a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/\">specification</a>).</p>\n<p>An <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-client\">A2A client</a> sends requests to an <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-remote-agent\">A2A server, the remote agent</a>, which it finds through its <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-agent-card\">Agent Card</a>. The specification has three layers. Its data model holds <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-task\">tasks</a>; messages, each one turn from the user or the agent; artifacts, the outputs of a task; and parts, the text, file or structured data inside messages and artifacts. Its operations send messages and get, list and cancel tasks. Its bindings, JSON-RPC 2.0, gRPC and HTTP+JSON/REST, carry them with the same behaviour, and custom bindings are allowed. Updates arrive by polling, by streaming or by push notifications to a webhook. The Protocol Buffers file <code>a2a.proto</code> is normative.</p>\n<p>A2A covers agents working with agents; <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">MCP</a> covers an agent using tools and data, and the two are complementary. Version 1.0 was released on 12 March 2026 and patch 1.0.1 on 28 May 2026, still the latest on 19 November 2026 (<a href=\"https://github.com/a2aproject/A2A/releases/tag/v1.0.1\">release</a>); patches do not affect compatibility (<a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/\">specification</a>).</p>\n<p>A2A's joining the Linux Foundation's Agentic AI Foundation (AAIF), as a Growth Stage project beside <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">MCP</a>, was announced on 27 August 2026 (<a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/blog/2026/08/27/a-new-chapter-for-a2a-joining-the-agentic-ai-foundation/\">A2A blog</a>).</p>",
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "spec.a2a.client",
          "anchor": "spec-a2a-client",
          "label": "Client, in the Agent2Agent protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-a2a-client",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/a2a/spec.a2a.client.md",
          "summaryHtml": "An A2A client lets an agent, or an application, hand work to another agent on a user's behalf. It is a role in the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a\">Agent2Agent protocol</a>.",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>An A2A client lets an agent, or an application, hand work to another agent on a user's behalf. It is a role in the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a\">Agent2Agent protocol</a>.</p>\n<p>The specification defines it as &quot;an application or agent that initiates requests to an A2A Server on behalf of a user or another system&quot; (<a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/\">specification</a>); its key concepts call it the client agent (<a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/key-concepts/\">key concepts</a>). It reads a <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-remote-agent\">remote agent</a>'s <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-agent-card\">Agent Card</a>, uses the first interface on it that it supports, sends messages, and follows the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-task\">tasks</a> that come back.</p>\n<p>It is not the <a href=\"/glossary/client/\">client</a> term's sense. An A2A client starts work with another agent, and is often an <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agent</a> itself; our client is a window onto a session an agent host owns. The two are related, not the same.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.a2a.remote-agent",
          "anchor": "spec-a2a-remote-agent",
          "label": "Remote agent, in the Agent2Agent protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-a2a-remote-agent",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/a2a/spec.a2a.remote-agent.md",
          "summaryHtml": "The remote agent lets work be handed to an agent elsewhere that keeps its own workings to itself. It is the server's role in the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a\">Agent2Agent protocol</a>.",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>The remote agent lets work be handed to an agent elsewhere that keeps its own workings to itself. It is the server's role in the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a\">Agent2Agent protocol</a>.</p>\n<p>The specification defines the A2A server (remote agent) as &quot;an agent or agentic system that exposes an A2A-compliant endpoint, processing tasks and providing responses&quot; (<a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/\">specification</a>). It must publish an <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-agent-card\">Agent Card</a>, and it runs the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-task\">tasks</a> its <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-client\">clients</a> ask for. To a client it is opaque, a black box whose memory and tools stay hidden (<a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/key-concepts/\">key concepts</a>); it may itself use <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-mcp\">MCP</a> to reach its tools.</p>\n<p>A remote agent is an <a href=\"/glossary/agent/\">agent</a> in its own right, or an <a href=\"/glossary/agentic-system/\">agentic system</a> of several, and not a <a href=\"/glossary/subagent/\">subagent</a>: it is independent of its client and opaque to it, where a subagent acts within its controlling agent's boundaries. The <a href=\"/glossary/remote-agent/\">remote agent</a> term rests on this card and means exactly what it means.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.a2a.agent-card",
          "anchor": "spec-a2a-agent-card",
          "label": "Agent Card, in the Agent2Agent protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-a2a-agent-card",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/a2a/spec.a2a.agent-card.md",
          "summaryHtml": "The Agent Card lets a client find an agent and know, before calling it, what it can do and how to reach it. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a\">Agent2Agent protocol</a>.",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>The Agent Card lets a client find an agent and know, before calling it, what it can do and how to reach it. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a\">Agent2Agent protocol</a>.</p>\n<p>It is a JSON document that every <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-remote-agent\">remote agent</a> must publish, describing its identity, capabilities, skills, service endpoint and authentication requirements (<a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/\">specification</a>). A <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-client\">client</a> finds it at the well-known address <code>https://{server_domain}/.well-known/agent-card.json</code>, in a registry or catalogue, or by direct configuration. It lists the agent's interfaces, each a binding and URL, in order of preference, and the client uses the first it supports. A card may be signed, and an agent may offer a fuller extended card to clients once they have authenticated.</p>\n<p>The card's skills are capabilities the agent declares, each with an id, name, description and tags. They are not skills in the Agent Skills sense, which extend an agent harness with knowledge and procedures; the two are related only by name.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "spec.a2a.task",
          "anchor": "spec-a2a-task",
          "label": "Task, in the Agent2Agent protocol",
          "status": "agreed",
          "url": "/specifications/#spec-a2a-task",
          "cardUrl": "https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agent-backpack/blob/75dd86fbff11a33b551fbd9d37b9d0576b1f9598/skills/keystone/ki-agentic-ecosystems/knowledge/specs/a2a/spec.a2a.task.md",
          "summaryHtml": "A2A's task lets a client follow work it has handed to a remote agent, however long it takes, through to its result. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a\">Agent2Agent protocol</a>.",
          "bodyHtml": "<p>A2A's task lets a client follow work it has handed to a remote agent, however long it takes, through to its result. It is part of the <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a\">Agent2Agent protocol</a>.</p>\n<p>A task is &quot;the fundamental unit of work managed by A2A, identified by a unique ID&quot; (<a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/\">specification</a>). A <a href=\"/specifications/#spec-a2a-remote-agent\">remote agent</a> answers a message with a message when it can reply at once, or with a task for longer work. A task moves through states: submitted and working; interrupted while it needs input or authentication; and finally completed, failed, cancelled or rejected. A finished task never restarts: a follow-up starts a new task in the same context (<a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/life-of-a-task/\">life of a task</a>). Its outputs are artifacts. A context, <code>contextId</code>, groups related tasks and messages, which the specification says should be treated as one conversational session.</p>\n<p>This graph uses &quot;task&quot; only as a vendor's or a specification's own word, never as a term, because vendors use it for a session, for delegated work and for scheduled work. A2A's task is delegated work, as the remote agent holds it. Its context comes nearest to a session, though what the remote agent keeps across a context is up to it.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "conforming": [
        {
          "slug": "copilot-studio",
          "name": "Copilot Studio",
          "vendor": "Microsoft"
        },
        {
          "slug": "gemini-cli",
          "name": "Gemini CLI",
          "vendor": "Google"
        },
        {
          "slug": "hermes-agent",
          "name": "Hermes Agent",
          "vendor": "Nous Research"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
